<div class="wp-block-group" style="min-height:460px"><!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained","contentSize":"680px","justifyContent":"left"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"color":{"text":"#ffffff"},"typography":{"fontWeight":"600","letterSpacing":"0.12em","textTransform":"uppercase"}},"fontSize":"xs"} -->
<p class="has-text-color has-xs-font-size" style="color:#ffffff;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase">Project — Adaptive reuse</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":1,"style":{"color":{"text":"#ffffff"},"typography":{"letterSpacing":"-0.02em","lineHeight":"1.02"}},"fontSize":"fluid-7-xl"} -->
<h1 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color has-fluid-7-xl-font-size" style="color:#ffffff;letter-spacing:-0.02em;line-height:1.02">Ferry Lane Boathouse</h1>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"color":{"text":"#ffffff"}},"fontSize":"xl"} -->
<p class="has-text-color has-xl-font-size" style="color:#ffffff">A shuttered ferry shed on the north bank, rebuilt as a rowing club, a boat workshop and a hall the town can book on a Tuesday night.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:cover -->

<!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"padding":{"right":"var:preset|spacing|5","left":"var:preset|spacing|5"},"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|8"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group" style="padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--5);padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--5)"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"lineHeight":"1.6"}},"fontSize":"xl"} -->
<p class="has-xl-font-size" style="line-height:1.6">The ferry stopped running in 1994 and the shed spent the next twenty-eight years holding scaffolding for other people's projects. The council owned it, the rowing club wanted it, and nobody could agree whether it was a building or a ruin with a roof. We were asked to settle that question in drawings before anyone spent money on it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontWeight":"600","letterSpacing":"0.12em","textTransform":"uppercase"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"xs"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-xs-font-size" style="font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase">The brief</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"}},"fontSize":"3-xl"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-3-xl-font-size" style="letter-spacing:-0.01em">A shed with one good wall</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">The survey took three weeks and produced one useful sentence: the river elevation, a hundred and forty feet of brick laid in 1911, was sound to within an inch. Everything behind it — the timber trusses, the concrete slipway, the two brick bays added in the sixties — had either rotted or been built badly enough that repairing it would cost more than starting again.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">That gave us a rule to design against rather than a style to argue about. Keep the wall, keep the slipway, keep the shape of the roof as the town remembers it from the far bank. Treat everything else as available. It also gave the club something to hold us to, which mattered more than we expected once the costings arrived.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:quote {"style":{"border":{"left":{"color":"var:preset|color|border","style":"solid","width":"3px"}},"spacing":{"padding":{"left":"var:preset|spacing|6"}}},"fontSize":"2-xl"} -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote has-2-xl-font-size" style="border-left-color:var(--wp--preset--color--border);border-left-style:solid;border-left-width:3px;padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--6)"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"lineHeight":"1.35"}}} -->
<p style="line-height:1.35">We did not want a landmark. We wanted somewhere the junior squad could drag a boat through wet kit at six in the morning without feeling they were spoiling it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Marta Oyelaran, Chair, Ferry Lane Rowing Club</cite></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:group {"style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}},"layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontWeight":"600","letterSpacing":"0.12em","textTransform":"uppercase"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"xs"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-xs-font-size" style="font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase">What we built</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"letterSpacing":"-0.01em"}},"fontSize":"3-xl"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-3-xl-font-size" style="letter-spacing:-0.01em">One room that changes twice a day</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">The boat bays sit at slipway level behind a sixty-foot sliding screen, so eights go straight out to the water without turning. Above them, on a new steel deck, the hall runs the full length of the brick wall with the old window openings unglazed at the top and shuttered in oak below. The club uses it for erg sessions before eight; after that it belongs to whoever booked it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"lg"} -->
<p class="has-lg-font-size">Servicing everything is a single lined block at the west end holding changing rooms, a kitchen and the plant. Putting the warm rooms in one insulated box let us leave the rest of the shed as a shed — unheated, ventilated through the ridge, dry enough for varnish work and cheap enough for a volunteer committee to run for the next forty years.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:image {"aspectRatio":"3/2","scale":"cover","width":"100%","sizeSlug":"large","style":{"border":{"radius":"8px"}}} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large is-resized has-custom-border"><img src="https://ui.shadcn.com/placeholder.svg" alt="The upper hall with the 1911 brick wall left exposed and oak shutters folded back from the window openings" style="border-radius:8px;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;width:100%"/><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">The upper hall on a weekday morning, three months after handover, with the shutters folded back along the river wall.</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"base"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-base-font-size">Completed 2025 for Ferry Lane Rowing Club with Tyne &amp; Wear District Council. Structural engineering by Halloran Reed; contractor, Bewick Marine Construction.</p>
